WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:26.000
If you can imagine it, there was a time before time. Well, before clock time, that is. In fact, there were many decades in colonial America when most people had no earthly idea what time it was. Most people, when they keep time, they just stick in the ground and call it noon. They look for the shadows. This is Michael Malley, a historian of time.
如果你能想象的话，在时间出现之前，曾有过一段“时间”。好吧，准确地说是时钟时间出现之前。事实上，在殖民地时期的美国，有几十年的时间，大多数人根本不知道现在几点。大多数人在计时的时候，只是在地上插根棍子，把它叫做正午。他们观察阴影。这位是时间历史学家迈克尔·马利。

2
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:46.000
What are you going to do when it's cloudy? Well, there's animal cues. You woke up with the rooster and at the end of the day. There's a phrase in American vernacular culture, till the cows come home. Well, the thing is, cows do go back to the barn at a certain time of day. So they could look outside and see the cows heading home, and they would know it was time for them to go home.
阴天的时候你该怎么办？嗯，还有动物的提示。你随着公鸡打鸣醒来，在一天结束时休息。美国俗语里有一句话，“直到奶牛回家”。嗯，事实是，奶牛确实会在一天中的特定时间回到牛棚。所以人们可以看看外面，看到奶牛往家走，他们就知道自己也该回家了。

3
00:00:46.000 --> 00:01:19.000
In the 1650s, the first public clocks were installed in Boston, Salem, and New York. And they were all the rage. New England towns would try to get a clock, and it was a sign of their kind of legitimate. it would be used to call people to prayer or, you know, call people to church or call the meeting. And with this clock tower, suddenly the clock was the center of everything. Being in earshot of the town bells marked the borders of civilization. If you were out of earshot of the town, you were almost in the wilderness.
19世纪50年代，第一批公共时钟被安装在波士顿、塞勒姆和纽约。它们风靡一时。新英格兰的城镇会努力争取安装一个时钟，这被视为一种合法性的标志。它会被用来召唤人们祈祷，或者说，召唤人们去教堂或参加会议。有了这座钟楼，时钟突然成了所有事物的中心。能听到城镇钟声的范围标志着文明的边界。如果你在城镇钟声的范围之外，你就几乎身处荒野了。

4
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:59.000
Life was becoming organized by the clock. But for the next 100 years, that clock said something different in every city. Noon was one thing in Boston. another in Providence, a third in New York, fine for a farmer, a nightmare for a railroad running on a schedule. So in 1883, the railroad barons got together and tamed this chaos, carving the country into four standard time zones. But for the average American worker, that didn't mean much, especially when your employer could still decide what time it was. The clock calls you to work. You still had to show up to the factory
生活开始被时钟组织起来。但在接下来的100年里，那个时钟在每个城市报出的时间都不一样。波士顿的正午是一个时间，普罗维登斯是另一个，纽约又是第三个，这对农民来说没问题，但对按时刻表运行的铁路来说简直是噩梦。因此在1883年，铁路大亨们聚在一起平息了这种混乱，将全国划分为四个标准时区。但对于普通美国工人来说，这意义不大，尤其是当你的雇主仍然可以决定现在几点的时候。时钟召唤你去工作。你仍然必须在老板敲钟时出现在工厂。

5
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:17.000
whenever the boss rang the bell. But people don't trust that because they feel like it's always being bumped ahead, you know, to get people in early. Everyone knew the foreman messed with the time to get more work out of you. They could change it. They could make it ahead in the morning and behind in the afternoon, right? They could change it. That's the issue.
无论老板什么时候敲钟。但人们并不信任它，因为他们觉得时间总是被提前，你知道的，为了让人们早点到。每个人都知道工头会搞乱时间来让你多干活。他们可以随意更改。他们可以在早上把时间调快，下午调慢，对吧？他们可以随意更改。这就是问题所在。

6
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:53.000
So you never know if that clock is accurate. And once you got inside, a human being was often the one deciding whether you were actually on time. So if you know, Joe, the guy checking you in, if he's your buddy, you're going to say, come on, Joe, give me a break. And he's going to mark you as on time when you're not. Or, you know, conversely, if Joe is the tool of management and eager to punish people he doesn't like, he can mark you as late when you're not. Everybody, employees and employers alike, thought this messiness of time was being used to cheat them.
所以你永远不知道那个时钟是否准确。一旦你进了工厂，往往是一个人来决定你是否真的准时。所以，如果你认识负责考勤的乔，如果他是你的哥们儿，你会说：“拜托，乔，通融一下。”他就会在你没准时的时候把你标记为准时。或者，反过来说，如果乔是管理层的走狗，急于惩罚他不喜欢的人，他可以在你没迟到的时候把你标记为迟到。每个人，无论是员工还是雇主，都认为这种时间的混乱被用来欺骗他们。

7
00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:27.000
And into this mess walked an unlikely hero, a man who didn't care much about the factory or power or money. He just cared about clocks. But the clock you built would do more than solve a workplace dispute. It would reorder America's relationship to time itself. From 99% Invisible and BBC Studios, this is a history of the United States in 100 objects. I'm Roman Mars. Today, the Bundy clock and how the first employee time clock
在这片混乱中，出现了一位不太可能的英雄，一个不太关心工厂、权力和金钱的人。他只关心时钟。但他制造的时钟所做的不仅仅是解决职场纠纷。它将重塑美国与时间本身的关系。来自《99%隐形》和BBC工作室，这是《100件物品中的美国史》。我是罗曼·马斯。今天，邦迪时钟以及第一台员工考勤钟如何引发了一场职场监控和效率的革命。记者艾莉·莱特富特带来了这个故事。我们这位不太可能的英雄，这位无意中改变了我们与时间关系的人，名叫威拉德·邦迪。威拉德出生于1845年，在纽约州奥本市长大，那是一个位于该州中心地带、规模虽小但蓬勃发展的工厂城镇。小时候，他是个工程天才。

8
