那天晚上，他们聚在一起达成了一项协议。他们说，下次泰勒主义者试图用秒表研究我们时，我们就停止工作。第二天，这件事就发生了。一个泰勒主义者拿着秒表走到一个工人面前，这次那个人只是拒绝配合。他立刻被解雇了，他的同事们看着这一切，在他身后走出了工厂。最终，工厂里的情况变得如此激烈，以至于在1912年，举行了一场全面的国会听证会来调查泰勒主义，以及他的效率方法是否在损害政府的工人。

51
00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:59.000
That night, they meet up and come to an agreement. They say, the next time a tailor right tries to study us with the stopwatch, we'll just stop working. And the very next day, that happens. a Taylorite comes up to a worker with a stopwatch, and this time the guy just refuses to go along with it. He gets fired immediately, and his fellow workers, watching all of this, walk out behind him. Eventually, things at the factory get so heated that in 1912, there's a full congressional hearing to investigate Taylorism, and whether his efficiency methods were damaging the government's workers.
工人们作证说：“我们不反对努力工作。我们为我们的技能感到自豪，我们为我们的工艺感到自豪，我们为我们完成工作的能力感到自豪。我们反对的是被秒表驱动。”这是罗曼在读他们说的真实话语。“我不反对他们找出需要多长时间，但我反对他们拿着秒表站在我身边，好像我是赛马或汽车一样。我们的反对意见是有人站在你身边像奴隶监工一样。秒表等同于鞭子。你还不如直接用那个。”

52
00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:35.000
Workers testify, we don't object to working hard. We are proud of our skill and we're proud of our craft and we're proud of our ability to get the job done. We do object to being driven by a stopwatch. This is Roman reading the actual words they said. I don't object to their finding out how long it takes, but I do object to there standing over me with a stopwatch as if I was a racehorse or an automobile. Our objection is to having someone standing over you as a slave driver. The stopwatch is equivalent to a whip. You might just as well have that.
工人们一次又一次地提到秒表是问题所在。正是这个物体夺走了他们的自由。一位工会领袖作证说：“它抹杀了机械师的个性。它只是让他成为机器的一部分。他不再为自己思考。他有别人为他思考。作为熟练工匠的意义将被实质性地贬低。”

53
00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:57.000
The workers again and again refer to the stopwatch as the problem. It was this object that was taking away their freedom. One union leader testified, It wipes out the individuality of a mechanic. It simply makes him part of a machine. He does not think for himself. He has others to think for him. The meaning of being a skilled artisan will have been materially degraded.
“你知道，我将无法控制我的劳动节奏。我将被变成一个机器般的实体。”当轮到泰勒主义者站上证人席时，他们并没有回避这一点。事实上，他们变本加厉。他们说，在我们的系统中，工人们是字面意义上的机器，正如其中一人所说。“人是一个便携式发电厂。他这里有一个锅炉，胃，消耗食物。这些过程与在炉排中燃烧煤炭没有什么不同。”

54
00:25:57.000 --> 00:26:24.000
You know, I won't have control over the pace in terms of my labor. I'll be made into a machine-like entity. When it's their turn to take the stand, the tailor rides don't shy away from this. Actually, they double down. They're like, in our system, working men are literal machines, as one of them put it. A man is a portable power plant. He has a boiler down here, the stomach, which consumes food. The processes are not dissimilar to burning coal in the grate.
“他这里有一个引擎，他的心脏，是一个泵。”但参议员们根本不吃这一套。他们因为愤怒而面红耳赤。你在证词中看不到这一点，因为它是经过编辑的，但他们互相咒骂，互相挥舞拳头。最终，工人们实际上赢了。泰勒主义和秒表在政府经营的工厂中被禁止，直到1949年。但对迈克来说，这次胜利并不是真正的胜利。

55
00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:52.000
He has an engine in here, his heart, which is a pump. But the senators are not having any of this. They're red-faced with rage at each other. You don't see this in the testimony because it's edited, but they're cursing at each other and shaking their fists at each other. In the end, the workers actually win. Taylorism and stopwatches are banned from government-run factories until 1949. But to Mike, this win wasn't really a win.
它代表了美国历史上的一个转折点，工人们停止了与机械时间本身的斗争，实际上，在某种程度上，接受了它。因为尽管他们说他们讨厌秒表，但兵工厂的工人们并没有试图摆脱它们或像第一批邦迪时钟的工人们那样破坏它们。事实上恰恰相反。为了对抗秒表，他们带来了自己的秒表。

56
00:26:52.000 --> 00:27:15.000
It represents this turning point in American history where workers stopped fighting against mechanical time itself and actually, in a way, kind of embraced it. Because as much as they said they hated the... the stopwatches, the workers at the arsenal weren't trying to get rid of them or vandalize them like workers had with the first Bundy clocks. It was actually the opposite. To fight the stopwatches, they brought their own.
没有办法绕过它，对吧？除了用时钟，没有办法对抗这个，你必须能够使用管理者的语言来对抗管理者。他们必须采用压迫者的语言来从压迫中解放自己，所以你真的自由了吗，对吧？我自由地打卡。就是这样。泰勒在沃特敦调查后仅几年就去世了，但他的门徒们还活着，并且传播着效率的福音，远远超出了工厂的围墙，

57
00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:48.000
There's no way around it, right? There's no way to combat this except with clocks, and you're going to have to be able to use the language of the manager to combat the manager. They have to adopt the language of the oppressor to free themselves from oppression, so are you really free, right? I'm free to punch a clock. that's that. Taylor died only a few years after the investigation at Watertown, but his disciples were alive and kicking, spreading the good word of efficiency far beyond the walls of the factory,
到办公室，到学校，到医院。事实上，哈佛商学院将其大部分早期课程建立在泰勒的思想之上。泰勒主义甚至被他的两个最亲密的门徒，一对名叫弗兰克和莉莲·吉尔布雷斯的夫妇带入了美国家庭。弗兰克和莉莲·吉尔布雷斯，他们是一对已婚夫妇。弗兰克是一名砖瓦承包商，但他沉迷于泰勒主义和他所谓的“唯一最佳方法”。宇宙中做任何工作都有一个“唯一最佳方法”。据说吉尔布雷斯是那个设置外科医生说“手术刀，镊子”，你知道，然后他们把它放在他手掌里的人。据说那是吉尔布雷斯。弗兰克崇拜泰勒，但正是他的妻子莉莲留下了真正的遗产。

58
00:27:48.000 --> 00:28:29.000
to offices, to schools, to hospitals. In fact, Harvard Business School built much of its early curriculum around Taylor's ideas. Taylorism was even brought into the American home by two of his closest disciples, a couple named Frank and Lily and Gilbreath. Frank and Lily and Gilbeth, they're a married couple. Frank is a brick contractor, but he becomes obsessed with Taylorism and what he calls the one best way. There's a one best way for doing any job in the universe. And supposedly Gilbert is the guy who sets up the thing where the surgeon goes scalpel, foreseps, you know, and they put it in his palm. Supposedly that's Gilbert. Frank worshipped Taylor, but it's his wife, Lillian, who left the real legacy.
莉莲·吉尔布雷斯实际上是家政学的创始人。莉莲对家庭所做的就像泰勒对工厂所做的那样。她进行了研究，为女性做鸡蛋、洗衣服、洗碗计时。她沉迷于最大化她所谓的“幸福分钟”。但正如你可以想象的那样……它很少减少工作量。工作只是转移到了其他任务上，或者对任务的期望提高了。最终，这确实也是泰勒主义的影响。

59
00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:59.000
Lillian Gilbert is actually sort of the founder of home economics. Lillian does for the home what Taylor does for the factory. She conducted studies where she timed women making eggs, doing laundry, doing the dishes. She was obsessed with maximizing what she called, quote, happiness minutes. But as you can imagine... Rarely does it decrease the amount of work. It just work moves to some other task or the expectations for the task go up. In the end, this really is the impact of Taylorism, too.
人们最终为了稍微多一点的钱做了更多的工作。我的意思是，关于泰勒的经典观察是，他有，我不知道，我编这些数字，但他有140人，假设，他把它减少到45人左右。那45人的工资稍微涨了一点，但涨幅没有管理层节省的钱多。所以他们为了稍微多一点的钱更努力地工作，而他们并没有实现休闲的梦想。如果你以前听说过效率福音，你可能知道这个想法，即洗碗机或洗衣机的发明，并没有突然让女性的生活多出几个小时。这是围绕人工智能的主要怀疑之一。

60
00:28:59.000 --> 00:29:41.000
People end up doing more work for slightly more money. I mean, the classic observation about Taylor is that he's got, I don't know, I'm going to make these numbers up, but he's got 140 people, let's say, and he reduces that to something like 45. And the wages of those 45 go up a little bit, but they don't go up as much as the savings to management. So they're working harder for slightly more money, and they don't realize the dream of leisure. If you've heard about the gospel of efficiency before, you're probably aware of this idea that the invention of the dishwasher or their laundry machine, didn't suddenly give women back hours of their lives. This is one of the major skepticism around AI.
人们最终为了多赚一点点钱而做了更多的工作。我的意思是，关于泰勒的经典观察是，他有，我不知道，我这些数字是编的，但他有140个人，比如说，他把人数减少到大约45人。这45人的工资确实涨了一点，但涨幅远不及管理层节省下来的成本。所以他们为了多赚一点点钱而更加努力地工作，却并没有实现休闲的梦想。如果你以前听说过效率福音，你可能知道这样一个观点，即洗碗机或洗衣机的发明并没有让女性突然多出几个小时的生活时间。这是围绕人工智能的主要怀疑论之一。

61
00:29:41.000 --> 00:30:06.000
It's like, okay, maybe these machines make us more efficient, but more efficient to do what exactly? Usually, the answer is more work. In a way, this is probably the realist, maybe the sneakiest legacy of Bundy and Taylor. Because at some point, this impulse towards surveillance and efficiency stopped coming from the bosses, and it started coming from us.
就像是，好吧，也许这些机器让我们更有效率，但更有效率到底是为了什么？通常答案是更多的工作。在某种程度上，这可能是邦迪（Bundy）和泰勒（Taylor）留下的最现实、也许也是最阴险的遗产。因为在某个时刻，这种对监控和效率的冲动不再来自老板，而是开始来自我们自己。

62
00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:26.000
The idea of self-regulation as a moral virtue is really important. You know, this is Weber's point, that we all become our own jailers. Why do I work? I have tenure. Why do I work hard? I'm my own jailer, right? It's a personal obligation to work. It's not that there's a guy standing next to me saying, work, you dog, right?
将自我调节视为一种道德美德的想法非常重要。你知道，这就是韦伯（Weber）的观点，我们都成了自己的狱卒。我为什么要工作？我有终身教职。我为什么要努力工作？我是我自己的狱卒，对吧？工作是一种个人义务。并不是旁边有个家伙站在那儿说，干活吧，你这只狗，对吧？

63
00:30:26.000 --> 00:31:04.000
I've internalized the impulse to work. It's become a moral imperative for me. And for most of us, people are. will work because they have to earn a living. They work because they have to make money. But there's also a sense that it's a duty and not working as a waste of your talents or it's sinful or it's sloth, right? The morality of working hard is not a new concept. The Protestant work ethic and all that has been around since the first settlers. But the difference now is that morality has been powered by clock time. You're constantly self-surveilling. There's a kind of identification of the person with their watch.
我已经内化了工作的冲动。它对我来说已经成为一种道德准则。对于我们大多数人来说，人们工作是因为他们必须谋生。他们工作是因为他们必须赚钱。但同时也有一种感觉，认为这是一种责任，不工作就是浪费你的才华，或者是有罪的，或者是懒惰的，对吧？努力工作的道德观并不是一个新概念。新教伦理等等自第一批定居者以来就一直存在。但现在的区别在于，这种道德观是由时钟时间驱动的。你一直在自我监控。人与他们的手表之间存在一种认同感。

64
00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:53.000
This idea that we've become identified with time, with our output, with how good we are based on how much we worked, it might seem like it snuck up on us. But if you look back at the ads from the very beginning of this industry, all the way back to the 1880s, you can see that this was kind of the point all along. Mike showed me one of these ads from 1888 that I think is kind of amazing. It's a cartoon of a little round, stout guy, and he's looking at his watch. The ad is made up of six little drawings of a man, the same man over and over again. In the first drawing, he's looking at his watch, looking normal. Then in the next figure, he's looking at his watch, but now he's a little bit rounder. And in the next one, he's even more round.
这种认为我们已经与时间、与我们的产出、与我们根据工作量来衡量自身价值的想法，似乎是悄悄地潜入我们的生活。但如果你回顾这个行业最初的广告，一直追溯到1880年代，你会发现这其实一直都是重点。迈克（Mike）给我看了其中一张1888年的广告，我觉得非常惊人。那是一幅卡通画，画着一个圆圆的、矮胖的家伙，他正在看他的手表。这则广告由六幅小图组成，画的是同一个人，一遍又一遍。在第一幅图中，他看着手表，看起来很正常。然后在下一幅图中，他看着手表，但现在他变得圆了一些。在下一幅图中，他变得更圆了。

65
00:31:53.000 --> 00:32:15.000
Then numbers appear on his face. Then the minute hand and the hour hand appear on his belly. And he's looking at his watch, and he habitually looks at his watch so often that eventually he turns into a watch. This is supposed to sell watches. This was an advertisement to sell the clock. This was supposed to get people in 1888 to say, yeah, I want that. Yeah, that was our first thought. It's monstrous, right.
然后数字出现在他的脸上。接着分针和时针出现在他的肚子上。他看着他的手表，他习惯性地频繁看表，以至于最终他变成了一块手表。这本意是为了推销手表。这是一则推销时钟的广告。这本意是让1888年的人们说，是的，我想要那个。是的，那是我们的第一个想法。这很可怕，对吧。

66
00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:45.000
But it's the safety and security of being somebody who's under the discipline of timekeeping. I think that's what they're offering. And we bought it because merging with a watch seemed like becoming something good. Well, you're not unruly. You're not one of the unruly classes. You're not one of those workers who they have to physically. surveil, you're not one of those workers who's truculent and potentially lazy, you're not disruptive, you're not going to be the subject of police interest, because it indicates your
但这就是成为一个受时间管理纪律约束的人所带来的安全感和保障。我想这就是他们所提供的。我们买了它，因为与手表融合似乎变成了一件好事。嗯，你不是不守规矩的。你不是那些不守规矩的阶层之一。你不是那些他们必须进行物理监控的工人之一，你不是那些倔强且可能懒惰的工人之一，你不是破坏者，你不会成为警察关注的对象，因为它表明了你的

67
00:32:45.000 --> 00:33:34.000
safety as a citizen of an industrial society. There's a merger of self and object that goes on, but the analogy to me would be to this compulsive checking of your devices, you know, always synchronizing yourself to the mothership. I want to go back to the brothers who started all of this. The brothers who began this revolution of mechanized time in the workplace, Willard and Harlow Bundy. Because their ending is kind of tragic and also kind of telling. These two brothers, Willard, the tinkerer who made the clock, Harlow, the businessman who figured out how to market it, they'd struck gold. But as the company expanded and became less about inventing and more about selling,
作为工业社会公民的安全性。这里发生了一种自我与物体的融合，但对我来说，类比就是这种对设备的强迫性检查，你知道，总是让自己与母舰同步。我想回到开启这一切的兄弟俩。开启这场工作场所机械化时间革命的兄弟，威拉德（Willard）和哈洛·邦迪（Harlow Bundy）。因为他们的结局有点悲剧，也有点发人深省。这两兄弟，威拉德是制造时钟的修补匠，哈洛是想出如何营销它的商人，他们发了大财。但随着公司扩张，重点不再是发明，而是销售，

68
00:33:34.000 --> 00:34:16.000
the brothers started to come apart. While Harlow gained more and more power, Willard was being system. systematically phased out. He went from superintendent of his own company to a lowly contract employee, working out of a room on the fifth floor that he had to rent. Willard's son even begged Harlow to pay Willard more money. He had approached Harlow one day and said, I think my father should be making much more money. These are his clocks, his inventions, his life's work. Harlow responds with, no, he's signed a contract. He's being paid adequately. Maybe Harlow started to see his brother as one of those slackers in the Bundy Clock ads,
兄弟俩开始分道扬镳。当哈洛获得越来越多的权力时，威拉德却被系统性地边缘化了。他从自己公司的负责人变成了卑微的合同工，在五楼一个他必须租用的房间里工作。威拉德的儿子甚至恳求哈洛给威拉德更多的钱。他有一天找到哈洛说，我认为我父亲应该赚更多的钱。这些是他的时钟，他的发明，他一生的心血。哈洛回应说，不，他签了合同。他得到的报酬是足够的。也许哈洛开始把他的兄弟看作是邦迪时钟广告中那些懒汉之一，

69
00:34:16.000 --> 00:34:45.000
just trying to cheat his boss out of more money. Whatever it was. The brother's feud went on for about two years, and Harlow eventually gets sick of it. So he ended up firing his own brother. Willard tries to start his own clock company, but Harlow sues him, over and over again. The brothers stayed in legal battles for the rest of his own. stood our lives and never again spoke in person.
只是想从老板那里骗更多的钱。不管是什么原因。兄弟俩的争执持续了大约两年，哈洛最终厌倦了。所以他最终解雇了自己的兄弟。威拉德试图创办自己的时钟公司，但哈洛一次又一次地起诉他。兄弟俩在余生中一直处于法律纠纷中，再也没有亲自交谈过。

70
00:34:45.000 --> 00:35:20.000
The lawsuits drain Willard's family of resources, and in 1907, only four years after striking out on his own, he dies. For Willard, the lifelong inventor, the craftsman hero of our story, for him to end up like this in a rented room on the fifth floor, irrelevant in his own company, I think you can argue that this is the outcome of the thing he invented, the employee time clock. Because whether he intended it or not, it started a process that helped strip the craftsmanship and skill from the hands of the working people.
诉讼耗尽了威拉德家庭的资源，1907年，在他自立门户仅四年后，他就去世了。对于威拉德，这位终身发明家，我们故事中的工匠英雄，最终落得如此下场，在五楼的一间出租屋里，在自己的公司里变得无关紧要，我认为你可以说这是他发明的产物，即员工打卡钟。因为无论他是否有意，它开启了一个过程，帮助剥夺了劳动人民手中的工艺和技能。

71
00:35:20.000 --> 00:36:28.000
It turned the men who made the machines into machines themselves. Meanwhile, Harlow stayed high up in the company. He retired, built a 10-bedroom palace in Pasadena, and left his wife multiple millions in today's dollars. The company, in the end, merged with a few others to become none other than IBM. Reporter and producer Ellie Lightfoot. A history of the United States and 100 Objects is a production of 99% Invisible and BBC Studios. It's hosted and curated by me, Roman Mars. This episode was produced by Ellie Lightfoot.
